clueweb09-en0001-13-08375.0 [Home (Joints)] []Home [] [] Joints [Disease] [Joint X-ray][Jugular] [] []Joints, particularly hinge joints like the elbow and the knee, are complex structures made up of bone, muscles, synovium and cartilage and ligaments, designed to bear weight and move the body through space. Joints can become painful for a number of reasons. The reasons can range from an injury to a specific joint to an overall condition, such as arthritis, that affects many joints in the body. What is going on in the body? Joints The joints are the areas between the bones that contribute to flexibility and movement. They consist of fluid, membranes, and cartilage. As a person ages, the fluid in the joints decreases. Our joints can crack either by force of manipulation or normal movement. Some people's joints crack when they walk up or down stairs, other people experience involuntary popping or cracking when they move their
clueweb09-enwp01-08-08858.0 Joint capability areas From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search The Joint Capability Areas (JCA) are a standardized set of definitions that cover the complete range of military activities. The system was initially established in May 2005 by the Joint Staff with input from each of the services. It is designed to establish "a new framework that paves the way for side-by-side comparisons of service contributions to joint warfighting and a tool that will assist decision-makers in deciding whether to move resources between service budgets."[1] The establishment of the JCA was primarily a result of an Operational Availability study from 2005 and from the "Joint Defense Capabilities Study"[1] of 2003 (which is almost universally referred to as the "Aldridge Study" after its primary author, Edward Aldridge.) The Aldridge study called for the establishment of a new joint lexicon that would allow leaders to clearly discuss mission areas and
